    {
      "skill_id": "fr-a1-010",
      "title": "Regular plurals and plural articles",
      "explanation": "Most French nouns form the plural by adding -s, which is silent in speech: le chat, les chats. Nouns that already end in -s, -x or -z do not change. Nouns ending in -eau add -x (le gâteau, les gâteaux) and most nouns ending in -al change to -aux (le journal, les journaux). The plural articles are the same for both genders: les (the) and des (some, or no article in English). In speech the article is what shows that a noun is plural.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "fr": "Les enfants jouent.",
          "en": "The children are playing."
        },
        {
          "fr": "Elle a des livres.",
          "en": "She has some books."
        },
        {
          "fr": "Les gâteaux sont ici.",
          "en": "The cakes are here."
        }
      ],
      "references": ["FEI-INV"],
