    {
      "skill_id": "fr-a1-011",
      "title": "Adjectives: agreement and position",
      "explanation": "French adjectives agree in gender and number with the noun they describe. Most add -e for the feminine and -s for the plural: petit, petite, petits, petites. Adjectives that already end in -e keep the same form in the feminine: rouge, rouges. Most adjectives come after the noun (une voiture rouge, un chat noir), but a few very common short ones come before it, for example petit, grand, bon and joli: une petite maison. After être the adjective follows the verb and still agrees with the subject: Les filles sont contentes.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "fr": "Il a un chat noir.",
          "en": "He has a black cat."
        },
        {
          "fr": "Nous avons une grande voiture.",
          "en": "We have a big car."
        },
        {
          "fr": "Les filles sont contentes.",
          "en": "The girls are happy."
        }
      ],
